{#
  The tenant CUDN with EVPN as its transport.

  Two things change relative to the phase-3 CUDN, and only two:

    transport: EVPN       - east/west pod traffic for this network stops
                            using Geneve and uses VXLAN instead, with the
                            EVPN control plane distributing reachability.
                            The UDN's addressing, isolation and role are
                            unchanged.

    evpnConfiguration     - which VTEP to use, and the VNI/route-target for
                            each VRF type.

  Layer3 topology takes ipVRF only - macVRF is for Layer2 networks, where the
  L2 segment itself is stretched across the fabric. A Layer2 tenant here
  would be the configuration to use if the goal were VM migration across the
  cluster boundary with the MAC and IP preserved; Layer3 is the right shape
  for routed tenant segmentation, which is what this lab's tenants are.

  routeTarget is set explicitly rather than left to default to
  <ASN>:<VNI>, because the fabric side has to match and an explicit value on
  both sides is one less thing to derive when a route is not appearing where
  it should. Two UDNs sharing a route target of the same VRF type would leak
  routes between tenants - which is exactly what this lab is trying to prove
  does not happen, so the values are distinct per tenant.
#}
apiVersion: k8s.ovn.org/v1
kind: ClusterUserDefinedNetwork
metadata:
  name: {{ tenant.name }}
  labels:
    {{ udn_bgp_cudn_label_key }}: "enabled"
    udn-lab-phase: evpn
spec:
  namespaceSelector:
    matchLabels:
      udn-tenant: {{ tenant.name }}
  network:
    topology: Layer3
    layer3:
      role: Primary
      subnets:
        - cidr: {{ tenant.udn_subnet }}
          hostSubnet: {{ tenant.udn_host_subnet }}
  transport: EVPN
  evpnConfiguration:
    vtep: evpn-vtep
    ipVRF:
      vni: {{ tenant.evpn_ip_vni }}
      routeTarget: "{{ clab_spine_asn }}:{{ tenant.evpn_ip_vni }}"
